test-branches: hand-written branch/loop instruction correctness on objtype=riscv64

WHAT IS TESTED
--------------
26 checks, all in branchtest.s (hand-written ja assembly, not C compiled
by ic) and called from main.c:

  - BEQ/BNE/BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU, 3-operand register-vs-register form
    (16 checks): for each of the four signed/unsigned-sensitive
    mnemonics (BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU), a "basic" case (3 vs 7, both
    positive) plus a "sign" case (-1 vs 1, and its operand-reversed
    twin) specifically chosen so a wrong operand order OR a signed/
    unsigned mix-up would flip the answer, not just happen to still
    look right. BEQ/BNE get a true and a false case each (signedness
    doesn't apply to equality).
  - BEQ/BNE/BLT/BGE, 2-operand-vs-implicit-zero form (8 checks): a
    different assembler grammar rule than the 3-operand form
    (cmd/ia/a.y: "LBEQ rreg ',' rel" vs "LBEQ rreg ',' sreg ',' rel"),
    tested separately since there's no reason to assume it shares
    whatever operand-order behavior the 3-operand form has.
  - Two loop-structure checks: loop_topguard_count reproduces the exact
    shape the original buggy 9/sun20i/l.s used (a leading guard branch,
    a body, and an unconditional JMP back to the guard) with small,
    hand-verifiable bounds. loop_bottomtest_count is the pattern l.s
    was fixed to use instead (a single trailing conditional branch, no
    leading guard), with different bounds than l.s happened to use, to
    confirm the fix generalizes rather than having only been checked
    once by luck.

WHY THIS CONSTRUCT
------------------
Every earlier test/driver in this project (the original 6-test/86-check
compiler suite, every rv_baremetal-boot-plan9-riscv64-* driver, the D1
diag probe) used either straight-line code or C-level if/switch/while
compiled BY ic - never a hand-written ja branch instruction. That gap
went unnoticed until 9/sun20i/l.s's BSS-zeroing loop: "BGE R9, R11,
target" assembled with its two register operands in the opposite order
from what was written, and a trailing unconditional JMP back to a label
preceded by that same conditional branch got rewritten into a different
conditional branch entirely (confirmed by disassembling the actual
built kernel - see PLAN9_D1_PORT_ROADMAP.md's Phase 1 section for the
full diagnosis). The fix that unblocked Phase 1 (a single trailing
BLT, no leading guard) was confirmed working exactly once, on exactly
one set of bounds, live inside real kernel bring-up. This test exists
to characterize the actual, general operand-order and loop-idiom rules
in isolation, since Phase 2 (trap dispatch) is about to depend on
hand-written conditional branches far more heavily than anything so
far - decoding scause into syscall/page-fault/timer/external-interrupt
is fundamentally a chain of compares-and-branches, and it deserves a
solid, separately-verified foundation rather than one lucky data point.

HOW TO READ THE RESULT
-----------------------
Watch UART0 (same console as every other rv_baremetal-boot-plan9-riscv64-*
example). Each of the 26 lines should read PASS - including
"loop_topguard_count (known-bad shape)", whose expected value (0, not
the naively-expected 5) matches what we now BELIEVE happens based on
the l.s diagnosis; a PASS there confirms that understanding, a FAIL
means something is inconsistent and needs more digging, not that the
test itself is wrong. A final line reads either "ALL TESTS PASSED" or
"SOME TESTS FAILED (see above)". The onboard LED (PIO bank D, pin 18 -
same GPIO code as rv_baremetal-boot-plan9-riscv64-test-switch, reused
unchanged, not reinvented) is a no-terminal fallback: solid on once the
test finishes means all 26 passed; a fast blink means at least one FAIL
was printed.

No watchdog feeding in this one, deliberately - same choice
rv_baremetal-boot-plan9-riscv64-test-switch already made: this is a
one-shot diagnostic, not something that needs to stay up. The UART
output has already been printed and read well before any watchdog
timeout would matter.

BUILD
-----
mk (produces main.out and main.bin, same convention as the other
rv_baremetal-boot-plan9-riscv64-* folders - main.bin is the flashable
headerless output, built with -s to strip Plan9 symbols).
